nio: flag whether the scheduler ever looked at the PID1 thread

CI 638 read PATH flags=0x50: the initial wakeup ran and the init thread
was made runnable, but it was never committed to a processor (0x20
absent).  Two more read-only bits in thread_select() separate the
remaining cases:

  bit 7  thread_select() ran at all (scheduler was entered)
  bit 8  at that moment the cloned PID1 thread was still TH_RUN and on
         a run queue

Together with the existing bits, the next photo distinguishes:

  no 0x100      the boot thread never entered the scheduler after exec
  0x100 only    scheduler ran but the init thread was not runnable then
  0x180         scheduler saw it runnable but chose another thread
  0x1A0 or more it was committed, so follow the TWR/URET/TER bits

No scheduling behaviour changes: the new code only sets sticky flags.


diff --git a/osfmk/kern/sched_prim.c b/osfmk/kern/sched_prim.c
index f124044..af4db5f 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/sched_prim.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/sched_prim.c
@@ -2443,6 +2443,13 @@ thread_select(thread_t          thread,
 	processor_set_t         pset = processor->processor_set;
 	thread_t                        new_thread = THREAD_NULL;
 
+	/* nio: read-only scheduler landmarks for the cloned PID1 thread. */
+	nio_user_path_flags |= 0x100;
+	if (nio_pid1_thread != THREAD_NULL &&
+	    (nio_pid1_thread->state & TH_RUN) && nio_pid1_thread->runq != PROCESSOR_NULL) {
+		nio_user_path_flags |= 0x80;
+	}
+
 	assert(processor == current_processor());
 	assert((thread->state & (TH_RUN | TH_TERMINATE2)) == TH_RUN);
 
